      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5 & k \\\\ -3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}|)+((-7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((7)\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot (-4)-(k)\\cdot (3)))+((-3)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-4)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((-7)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (3)-(-5)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((7)\\cdot (20-3k))+((-3)\\cdot (3k-12))+((-7)\\cdot (-6))\\\\ &=(140-21k)+(36-9k)+(42)\\\\ &=218-30k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}218-30k&=0\\\\ -30k&=-218\\\\ k&=\\frac{109}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{109}{15}.$",
