      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & -7 \\\\ -4 & k & 3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 \\\\ k & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7 \\\\ -4 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot ((-7)\\cdot (3)-(-7)\\cdot (k)))+((4)\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot (3)-(-7)\\cdot (-4)))+((-4)\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot (k)-(-7)\\cdot (-4)))\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot (7k-21))+((4)\\cdot (-43))+((-4)\\cdot (-5k-28))\\\\ &=(42k-126)+(-172)+(20k+112)\\\\ &=62k-186\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62k-186&=0\\\\ 62k&=186\\\\ k&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=3.$",
