      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -6 & k \\\\ -8 & -6 & -7\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & k \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}|)+((7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -7\\end{aligned}|)+((7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((-6)\\cdot (-7)-(k)\\cdot (-6)))+((7)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-7)-(k)\\cdot (-8)))+((7)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-6)-(-6)\\cdot (-8)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (6k+42))+((7)\\cdot (8k+14))+((7)\\cdot (-36))\\\\ &=(12k+84)+(56k+98)+(-252)\\\\ &=68k-70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}68k-70&=0\\\\ 68k&=70\\\\ k&=\\frac{35}{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{35}{34}.$",
