      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 7 & k & -2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(4)\\cdot (k)))+((-7)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-2)-(4)\\cdot (7)))+((-7)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (k)-(2)\\cdot (7)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (-4k-4))+((-7)\\cdot (-38))+((-7)\\cdot (5k-14))\\\\ &=(-12k-12)+(266)+(98-35k)\\\\ &=352-47k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}352-47k&=0\\\\ -47k&=-352\\\\ k&=\\frac{352}{47}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{352}{47}.$",
