      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ -7 & -7 & k \\\\ 6 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & k \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & k \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((6)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((-7)\\cdot (-3)-(k)\\cdot (2)))+((-7)\\cdot ((-7)\\cdot (-3)-(k)\\cdot (6)))+((6)\\cdot ((-7)\\cdot (2)-(-7)\\cdot (6)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (21-2k))+((-7)\\cdot (21-6k))+((6)\\cdot (28))\\\\ &=(4k-42)+(42k-147)+(168)\\\\ &=46k-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}46k-21&=0\\\\ 46k&=21\\\\ k&=\\frac{21}{46}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{21}{46}.$",
