      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & k\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot (k)-(-2)\\cdot (4)))+((2)\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot (k)-(-2)\\cdot (6)))+((-8)\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot (4)-(-5)\\cdot (6)))\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot (8-5k))+((2)\\cdot (12-5k))+((-8)\\cdot (10))\\\\ &=(20k-32)+(24-10k)+(-80)\\\\ &=10k-88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10k-88&=0\\\\ 10k&=88\\\\ k&=\\frac{44}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{44}{5}.$",
